WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.967
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.549
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:15.542
Unit nineteen: Digital Humanities. Today you will discuss future possibilities in research with 'could'.

4
00:00:15.842 --> 00:00:18.540
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.197
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.620
Digital humanities.

7
00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:31.739
Digital humanities means the use of digital tools to study history, literature and culture.

8
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.313
Digital humanities projects map ancient trade.

9
00:00:35.917 --> 00:00:39.266
Your turn. Say: Digital humanities.

10
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.670
Digital humanities.

11
00:00:44.417 --> 00:00:45.987
Data visualization.

12
00:00:46.983 --> 00:00:50.945
Data visualization means presenting data as charts, maps or images.

13
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.165
Data visualization revealed hidden patterns.

14
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.785
Your turn. Say: Data visualization.

15
00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:02.168
Data visualization.

16
00:01:02.917 --> 00:01:04.045
Text mining.

17
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:09.244
Text mining means using software to analyse large amounts of text.

18
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:13.510
Text mining traced key words across 10,000 newspapers.

19
00:01:14.117 --> 00:01:17.082
Your turn. Say: Text mining.

20
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:21.016
Text mining.

21
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.227
Digital archive.

22
00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:27.435
Digital archive means a collection of records stored online.

23
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.920
A digital archive preserves fragile inscriptions.

24
00:01:31.517 --> 00:01:34.633
Your turn. Say: Digital archive.

25
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.861
Digital archive.

26
00:01:39.617 --> 00:01:41.512
Computational analysis.

27
00:01:42.517 --> 00:01:47.395
Computational analysis means analysis performed with computers and algorithms.

28
00:01:47.983 --> 00:01:50.854
Computational analysis dated the manuscripts.

29
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.205
Your turn. Say: Computational analysis.

30
00:01:58.067 --> 00:01:59.880
Computational analysis.

31
00:02:00.617 --> 00:02:01.525
Mapping.

32
00:02:02.517 --> 00:02:06.072
Mapping means creating maps to display spatial information.

33
00:02:06.683 --> 00:02:09.124
Mapping showed the extent of Angkor's suburbs.

34
00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:12.079
Your turn. Say: Mapping.

35
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:15.876
Mapping.

36
00:02:16.617 --> 00:02:17.664
Metadata.

37
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:21.242
Metadata means data that describes other data.

38
00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.314
Good metadata makes documents searchable.

39
00:02:24.917 --> 00:02:27.499
Your turn. Say: Metadata.

40
00:02:30.333 --> 00:02:31.416
Metadata.

41
00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:33.523
Accessibility.

42
00:02:34.517 --> 00:02:38.513
Accessibility means how easily people can use or reach something.

43
00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:41.941
Online archives improve accessibility.

44
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.353
Your turn. Say: Accessibility.

45
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:49.665
Accessibility.

46
00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:54.593
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:59.226
Visal, how has technology changed what we know about Angkor?

48
00:03:00.317 --> 00:03:11.920
Enormously. LiDAR scans revealed a vast urban network hidden under the forest, which shows that digital tools could transform historical research.

49
00:03:13.017 --> 00:03:15.005
What else could be done in future?

50
00:03:16.117 --> 00:03:24.989
Text mining could analyse thousands of Khmer inscriptions at once, and mapping could show how temple networks changed over centuries.

51
00:03:26.083 --> 00:03:27.328
Are there risks?

52
00:03:28.417 --> 00:03:36.990
Yes. Data could be misread without historical context, and archives could be lost if no one maintains the servers.

53
00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:39.738
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.583 --> 00:03:43.941
Grammar focus: Future possibility with 'could'.

55
00:03:44.241 --> 00:03:45.370
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:46.083 --> 00:03:48.954
Digital tools could transform historical research.

57
00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:53.289
Text mining could analyse thousands of inscriptions at once.

58
00:03:54.183 --> 00:03:57.181
Archives could be lost if servers are not maintained.

59
00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:09.217
'Could' expresses a future or theoretical possibility that is less certain than 'will'. Use 'could be + p.p.' for passive possibilities and add 'potentially' or 'in theory' for extra caution.

60
00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:14.238
Be careful! Don't say: Digital tools could transforming research.

61
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.500
Say: Digital tools could transform research.

62
00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.171
After 'could', use the base verb.

63
00:04:22.967 --> 00:04:25.387
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.049
History in the Digital Age.

65
00:04:28.349 --> 00:04:34.922
Digital humanities applies computational methods to questions traditionally studied by historians and literary scholars.

66
00:04:35.933 --> 00:04:41.288
Aerial laser scanning has already transformed our understanding of Angkor's size and population.

67
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:48.142
In the future, machine learning could help to read damaged inscriptions that human experts find illegible.

68
00:04:49.133 --> 00:04:55.905
Digital archives could also give students in rural areas access to sources once available only in distant capitals.

69
00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:02.742
However, these tools could mislead researchers if numbers are interpreted without careful attention to context.

70
00:05:03.833 --> 00:05:07.731
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:08.583 --> 00:05:11.488
AI blank help historians read damaged texts.

72
00:05:11.788 --> 00:05:15.372
A, could. B, could to. C, coulds.

73
00:05:18.933 --> 00:05:20.515
The answer is A: could.

74
00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.149
Many records blank lost without backups.

75
00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:27.649
A, could be. B, could. C, could being.

76
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.292
The answer is A: could be.

77
00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.030
Data that describes other data is blank.

78
00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:41.134
A, text mining. B, metadata. C, mapping.

79
00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.607
The answer is B: metadata.

80
00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.458
Presenting data as charts and maps is blank.

81
00:05:50.758 --> 00:05:55.642
A, data visualization. B, accessibility. C, digital archive.

82
00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.012
The answer is A: data visualization.

83
00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:07.228
According to the reading, what has transformed understanding of Angkor?

84
00:06:07.528 --> 00:06:12.029
A, aerial laser scanning. B, text mining. C, metadata.

85
00:06:15.567 --> 00:06:18.344
The answer is A: aerial laser scanning.

86
00:06:19.533 --> 00:06:22.453
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:23.317 --> 00:06:26.907
How might digital tools change the way historians study the past?

88
00:06:43.717 --> 00:06:45.112
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:45.412 --> 00:07:21.486
Digital tools could let historians examine far more evidence than any person could read alone. Text mining, for example, could reveal patterns across thousands of newspapers or inscriptions in a few hours. In Cambodia, LiDAR scanning has already shown that Angkor was a huge urban network, not just a group of temples. However, some scholars warn that data could be misinterpreted without careful reading. I believe the best results will come when digital methods support, not replace, traditional historical skills.

90
00:07:21.786 --> 00:07:23.530
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:24.417 --> 00:07:28.915
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and future possibility with 'could'.

92
00:07:29.215 --> 00:07:34.131
Today's value is innovation. Try new ideas boldly, and learn from every attempt.

93
00:07:34.431 --> 00:07:36.921
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

94
00:07:37.221 --> 00:07:40.071
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

